# file: requirements.md
# Pomodoro + Calendar + Notes — Requirements (v2)

A single-user productivity app. The home page is a **month calendar**; each
event on the calendar carries its own pomodoro configuration. A floating
widget in the bottom-right lets the user start an ad-hoc pomodoro without
touching the calendar and always shows the currently-running timer.

## Primary flows

### 1. Schedule an event
- Home shows a month grid; today and days-with-events are marked.
- Click a day → a **modal** (Google-Calendar-style) opens with:
  - **Title** (required)
  - **Description** (multi-line)
  - **Start time** (date + HH:MM; date pre-fills from the clicked day)
  - **Advanced settings** (collapsed by default, toggle open):
    - Work cycle: `work_min` (default 25)
    - Break: `break_min` (default 5)
    - Cycles: `cycles` (default 1)
- Save creates the event; the day cell now shows an event dot.
- Modal also has a **Start now** button that immediately kicks off this
  event's pomodoro sequence, in addition to Save.

### 2. Run a pomodoro (from an event)
- On the event details modal (open by clicking an existing event), press
  play. The floating widget starts counting down `work_min:00`.
- After work, auto-transitions to break for `break_min:00`, then next work
  cycle, until `cycles` are done.
- During any cycle the user can add **session notes** — free-text lines
  appended to the event's note log.

### 3. Run a pomodoro (ad-hoc, no calendar event)
- Click the idle floating widget → small popover form:
  - Title (required)
  - Start with defaults (25 / 5 / 1)
- An event is still created (dated `now`) so the notes have somewhere to
  live; the calendar reflects it.

### 4. View / calendar toggle
- Header has **Month | Week** toggle.
- Month view: 6×7 grid (always 6 rows, days from prev/next month greyed).
- Week view: 1×7 columns for the week containing `today`, with hour rows
  (08–20) and events positioned by start-time.

### 5. Settings
- Gear icon in header opens a settings modal:
  - **Idle text**: the string the floating widget shows when nothing runs
    (default `( :3 )=3`).
- Stored on the server as a singleton `Settings` node (survives reloads,
  syncs across tabs — simpler than localStorage for a single-user app).

## UI components

| Component        | Purpose                                                     |
|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
| `AppShell`       | Header (title, view toggle, gear) + main + floating widget. |
| `MonthCalendar`  | 6×7 grid; event dots; click a day → open EventEditor.       |
| `WeekCalendar`   | 7-column × 13-hour grid; events as time-positioned blocks.  |
| `EventEditor`    | Modal for create OR edit an event; contains AdvancedFields. |
| `AdvancedFields` | Collapsible: work / break / cycles number inputs.           |
| `EventDetails`   | Modal shown when clicking an existing event (view + Play).  |
| `PomodoroFloat`  | Bottom-right fixed widget; two states.                      |
|                  | · Idle: shows `settings.idle_text`; click → StartAdhocForm. |
|                  | · Running: shows MM:SS; click → expands to PomodoroDetails. |
| `PomodoroDetails`| Timer + title + description + notes list + note input +     |
|                  | play/pause/stop controls (three icon buttons).              |
| `StartAdhocForm` | Popover on idle widget: title + Start.                      |
| `SettingsModal`  | Idle-text field + Save.                                     |

## Backend

Graph:
```
Root ──> Event(year, month, day, hour, minute, title, description,
               work_min, break_min, cycles)
              ──> Note(text, created_at)
Root ──> Session(event_id, started_at, phase, cycle_ix, remaining_sec,
                 running, paused_at)
Root ──> Settings(idle_text)                    (singleton)
```

- `Session.phase` ∈ `"work" | "break" | "done"`.
- `Session.cycle_ix` counts completed work cycles (0..cycles-1).
- Timer is **derived on the client** from
  `started_at + elapsed_since - paused_offset`, so no server tick.
- Only one running Session at a time; new starts stop the old one.

Public walkers (`walker:pub`, anonymous — single-user app):

| Walker                | Args                                       | Returns          |
|-----------------------|--------------------------------------------|------------------|
| `list_events`         | `year`, `month`                            | `list[Event]`    |
| `get_event`           | `event_id`                                 | `Event`          |
| `create_event`        | full event fields                          | `Event`          |
| `update_event`        | `event_id` + fields                        | `Event`          |
| `add_note`            | `event_id`, `text`                         | `Note`           |
| `list_notes`          | `event_id`                                 | `list[Note]`     |
| `start_pomodoro`      | `event_id`                                 | `Session`        |
| `pause_pomodoro`      | `session_id`                               | `Session`        |
| `resume_pomodoro`     | `session_id`                               | `Session`        |
| `stop_pomodoro`       | `session_id`                               | `Session`        |
| `advance_phase`       | `session_id`                               | `Session`        |
| `current_session`     | —                                          | `Session\|None`  |
| `get_settings`        | —                                          | `Settings`       |
| `set_idle_text`       | `text`                                     | `Settings`       |

`advance_phase` is called by the client when the derived timer hits 0:
server flips phase/cycle_ix and returns the updated Session so the client
resyncs (avoids clock drift and races between tabs).

## Non-goals (V1)

- No multi-user auth. Everything is anonymous on the shared root.
  (Switching walkers to `:priv` later gives each user their own root.)
- No cross-month navigation in the calendar (fixed to `today.month`).
- No drag-to-reschedule.
- No browser notifications on phase transition (visual only).
- No delete-event flow (add later).

